Hey Clark,

48
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
And Walter Underwood is out there in the tall trees.

49
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:55,000
The Palo Alto, California.

50
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:57,000
How you doing, Walter?

51
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:58,000
I'm doing great

52
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,000
High-climb.

53
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,000
We're moving right along here.

54
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:11,000
I had an email question that I thought would be a good topic for discussion with the panel

55
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,000
And let me read the email to you. fellas,

56
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:24,000
I'm a new scout master of six months and have only two years as a volunteer adult with the Boy Scouts of America and no prior experiences in scouting.

57
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:38,000
I just returned from a committee meeting where members complained that some of the scouts need to be punished for using their electronic devices to play games during yesterday's advancement meeting.

58
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,000
One adult suggested I make the scouts do push-ups as a punishment.

59
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:48,000
Others thought a type of reprimand or demerit system should be put in place.

60
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:57,000
I told the members that there was already a rule for scouts not to use electronic devices during the meetings and that the patrol leaders' counsel should have handled the issue.

61
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:05,000
Unfortunately, the senior patrol leader and the other leaders did not do anything to stop the scouts from playing games on their devices

62
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:09,000
And the behavior continued for about half an hour.

63
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:13,000
What would be an appropriate way to deal with this in the future?

64
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:19,000
Can the committee install a type of discipline or demerit system I would have to enforce?

65
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:25,000
Thank you for your help in this relatively simple but now somewhat complex issue.

66
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:31,000
That is a bit of a can of worms there. Who wants to get started?

67
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:36,000
There's three issues that you have to deal with. The first one is electronic devices.

68
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,000
You've got to talk about that. And then the second thing is punishment,

69
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:43,000
And I think that's the thing that you want to talk about.

70
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:51,000
But the third thing to talk about is that last line and that's the committee scout master relationship.

71
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,000
And I'll just start with that one just for a second and then let you guys go.

72
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:59,000
But no, can the committee do that?

73
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:03,000
No, The scout master is in charge of the program.

74
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:07,000
The committee chairman can certainly talk to the scout master,

75
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:13,000
But if it's not in the scout handbook and it doesn't say here's how the Boy Scout demerit system worked,

76
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:17,000
then no, the committee can't do that if the scout master and the SPL don't want to do that.

77
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:25,000
The committee chairman says: you know, you sent us a guy for a board and you didn't do a scout master conference.

78
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,000
The scout master says: eh, you know why do we do that.

79
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:34,000
Then he can say: wait, here's the book. But in this case scouting doesn't have a demerit system.

80
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:42,000
So no, he can't say that. So it's really up to the SPL, with the advisement of the scout master.

81
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:47,000
And it's a bad idea. But can the committee chairman do that? No,

82
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Let's concentrate on that question for a moment.

83
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:54,000
Is it within the purview of the committee chairman to be able to set something like that

84
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:57,000
and tell the scout master it's time for him to enforce it?

85
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:04,000
Well, that was my first response too. It's like no, the committee cannot set up a demerit system.

86
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:10,000
The committee can talk to the scout master about the program,

87
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:19,000
But the scout master says: we are teaching the SPL and the patrol leaders how to lead.

88
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:26,000
And then the scout master teaches the SPL and patrol leaders: here's what you do.

89
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:30,000
So these guys are not paying attention, They're doing this and this

90
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:36,000
You need to spread out. And the next time scout master taps somebody on the shoulder,

91
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:44,000
takes them over and says whisper, whisper, whisper, let's you know, go settle this down.

92
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
so the guys are paying attention.

93
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:54,000
I say no to that also, And there have been times when I have noticed some of the youth,

94
00:06:54,000 --> 00:07:00,000
some of the older scouts, doing something over on the side with a phone,

95
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:04,000
And I'll try and get the same patrol leaders' attention and point to it.

96
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
He may or may not go over and say something

97
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,000
And it'll go on for a little bit

98
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:13,000
And I'll sort of walk over there and look over the shoulder and see what's going on.

99
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:17,000
And they're looking up something that pertains to scouting on the phone.

100
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:19,000
That always bothers me too,

101
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,000
But you're old.

102
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:22,000
Yeah,

103
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:26,000
In consultation with the patrol leaders' council about dealing with this,

104
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:28,000
because it's a problem for everybody,

105
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:33,000
They knew if somebody was using a cell phone or an electronic device

106
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:39,000
in aid of what was happening or if they were just being distracted by it,

107
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:43,000
and that if they judge that somebody is being distracting

108
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:48,000
and their encouragements or directions to them go unheeded,

109
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:51,000
that that person needs to come to talk to me.

110
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:57,000
Do you guys have any specific way of handling things like that?

111
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:01,000
Well, now we're on to the first question that I listed, which is the devices,

112
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:04,000
And I disagree with you when you say everybody has a problem,

113
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:06,000
because I just ignore it.

114
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:14,000
I know on the internet I've seen conversations on various places

115
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:17,000
and everybody gets their drawers in a knot about this whole thing.

116
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:22,000
And I've ignored the whole thing because I truly believe that those guys are going to lose.

117
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:25,000
You cannot go backwards.

118
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:28,000
This thing is part of life.

119
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:31,000
They're going to have to learn how to pay attention

120
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:35,000
and get everything done, just like they did with other distractions in the world,

121
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:37,000
And I don't know how that's going to work out in the end.

122
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:40,000
but I just don't think there's any way.

123
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:47,000
I think making a bunch of rules and getting all involved in this is the wrong thing to do.

124
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:50,000
Now the scouts need to enforce amongst themselves

125
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:55,000
how they're going to deal with scouts that have an attention problem

126
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,000
and let them work it out.

127
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:02,000
But I think if you stick your nose in it and you get all spun up about it,

128
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:05,000
eventually those guys are just going to vote with their feet.

129
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
And those families that say:

130
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:10,000
what do you mean? my kid can't have a phone on the camp out.

131
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,000
That's crazy. Bye, I'm leaving.

132
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:14,000
That's their family rule.

133
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,000
I can't make a battle out of it.

134
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:18,000
Let me tell you this? I don't think we disagree.

135
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:20,000
I agree with everything you've said.

136
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:24,000
What I described is the way that they handle any kind of a discipline or difficulty.

137
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,000
If they can't correct it, I'm the next step.

138
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,000
Walter, what about you?

139
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:33,000
I'm a digital native, so everybody's got a phone.

140
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:41,000
Our guys, we say, maybe in the car you can chat, but put it down,

141
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
Let's go camping.

142
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:47,000
Will you go camping where there's no reception anyway?

143
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:51,000
Yeah, that's, what we've got is no reception.

144
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
Most of their parents told them to take it.

145
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:57,000
They've got it but they can't use it because there is no reception.

146
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:01,000
I don't get in a knot over them doing it,

147
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,000
I just sort of make it a.

148
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:07,000
If I see it. I'll just make a little point about respect

149
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:11,000
towards the same trollator and anything constructive right now,

150
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:17,000
But anyway, I don't really get upset about it.

151
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:19,000
That's one of the few things I guess.

152
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
Well, I'll tell you an interesting story.

153
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,000
When we got down to summer camp a year or so ago,

154
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
we were getting set up,

155
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:27,000
My parents were all over in their area

156
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:31,000
and one of my assistant scoutmasters got a phone call

157
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,000
and his phone started ringing there in the campsite.

158
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:36,000
He took it out and he looked at it and he said:

159
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:39,000
wow, I didn't know that I was going to get reception here,

160
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:42,000
and they must have changed the towers or something around camp.

161
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:45,000
He took the call and it was his wife, and he said:

162
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:49,000
yeah, honey, I can get a call right here in the campsite.

163
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:53,000
and then a look of horror spread over his face

164
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:57,000
And he really wanted to take those words back,

165
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:06,000
But for the rest of the week he just turned his phone off.

166
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:10,000
Well, our summer camp is up in the Sierras

167
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,000
and it's really it's kind of remote.

168
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:17,000
There was a cell phone bench so you can go up and around

169
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
off the trail and sit there,

170
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:22,000
and that had better cell phone reception.

171
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:26,000
When we're on long trips traveling,

172
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:30,000
having them in the cars helps keep the noise down to some extent,

173
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:36,000
But I like to use the scouts like if I have something

174
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:38,000
I would really like to tell a car, that's, you know,

175
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:42,000
the driver or another adult, to send the car behind me

176
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:45,000
or in front of me. get the scouts to be the relays,

177
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:50,000
Let them do the communicating between vehicles, and that works.

178
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
Yeah, we've done that.

179
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
What I think Clark really wants to talk about, though,

180
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:55,000
is item number two.

181
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:59,000
Yeah, and that's punishment

182
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
All right. so let's talk about that for a moment.

183
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:05,000
One person on the committee suggested doing push-ups.

184
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:06,000
How about that?

185
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:08,000
Let's just talk about that real quick.

186
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:12,000
As adults we have never, never on anything,

187
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:15,000
had any kind of punishment like that.

188
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:18,000
We've given them stuff to think about.

189
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:24,000
But I tell you, it's the youth that came up with push-ups,

190
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:27,000
especially like clearing camp before we're leaving

191
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
and making sure everything's picked up.

192
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:32,000
We tell them to sweep the camp

193
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,000
and then we'll come and look for trash

194
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,000
and see if we can find any after they do

195
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,000
and they go. what if you do?

196
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
And we all stand there and look at them and go.

197
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:43,000
I don't know what if we do push-ups

198
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:47,000
And we go. sure, if you want to, that's fine with us.

199
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:50,000
And we've never left a camp that they didn't have to do some.

200
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
but it's always on their terms.

201
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:53,000
They do it.

202
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:56,000
We're not making them do that.

203
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
Walter, what do you think about push-ups?

204
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:01,000
I'm a little iffy on the whole push-ups thing,

205
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:05,000
but hey, our guys do. if you're not in uniform,

206
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:08,000
as defined by the SPL,

207
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:11,000
then push-ups are. if you're not on time, push-ups.

208
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:15,000
And you know, I showed up in civilian clothes

209
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:18,000
to drop off some forms last night

210
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:23,000
and I ran out before. I had some push-ups,

211
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:28,000
But once, once everyone was in uniform

212
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:31,000
and the senior patrol did push-ups.

213
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
Well now, if the scouts but notice the sentence

214
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:37,000
it says, one adult suggested I, the scout master,

215
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:40,000
make the scouts do push-ups.

216
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:43,000
That's a different issue than what Walter's talking about.

217
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:47,000
when the scouts get together and do some you know

218
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:52,000
Yippee-ki-yay thing, some moral morale boosting thing,

219
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:53,000
That's different.

220
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:56,000
Let me be very specific about the question.

221
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:59,000
So I'll start with you, Larry.

222
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:06,000
Is it proper for an adult to impose punishment

223
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:09,000
through some kind of forced physical action like push-ups?

224
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:10,000
No,

225
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:11,000
Okay,

226
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:12,000
That's baloney.

227
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:13,000
No,

228
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:14,000
Yeah,

229
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:15,000
And Walter.

230
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
Walter says no.

231
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:20,000
So I'm kind of iffy about the scouts doing it.

232
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:23,000
I've let it ride. but

233
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:27,000
Well, I did check in the Scout Master's Handbook

234
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:30,000
because I remembered something specific about this,

235
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:33,000
And it will tell you very directly:

236
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:38,000
adults are not to impose that type of forced physical

237
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:41,000
punishment on anyone ever.

238
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:44,000
The situations you described, where this is initiated

239
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:47,000
by the scouts, I feel like you do, Walter.

240
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:50,000
I'm a little iffy about that because I can see it.

241
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,000
It can head over the edge real quick.

242
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:54,000
There's a game aspect to it

243
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:59,000
If they're initiating it. I'm going to side on the safe side

244
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
because we're advising people.

245
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:04,000
You would need to think long and hard about that

246
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:08,000
and you need to observe the prohibition against such things

247
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:10,000
that you'll find in scouting literature.

248
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:13,000
You can normally tell if a Scout Master's on the ball

249
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
and he's paying attention.

250
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:19,000
He can tell the difference between a bunch of gung-ho guys

251
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:24,000
and a 15, probably 16 or 17-year-old ROTC kid

252
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:27,000
riding herd on a bunch of 11-year-olds,

253
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:30,000
which is just as bad as the adults doing it.

254
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:33,000
So there's a difference and you can tell it

255
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
and you can feel it.

256
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:40,000
If it gets to the point where the PLC is up there

257
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:43,000
doing push-ups and then after they're done, they all cheer,

258
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:49,000
you can feel the difference between that and the other thing,

259
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:52,000
which is you guys get down on the floor.

260
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:55,000
There's a line and I think you've got to watch that.

261
00:15:55,000 --> 00:16:01,000
Maybe I'll suggest the SPL, the senior patrol, does push-ups

262
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:05,000
unless everyone is in uniform.

263
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,000
Yes,

264
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
Ours is all in fun.

265
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
It's not the finger-pointing,

266
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
It's everybody. and, like Walter said,

267
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
it doesn't matter whether you can get up on the floor.

268
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:19,000
you're trying and it truly is all in fun

269
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:21,000
and we watch for that.

270
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:28,000
It's not standing over and making them do whatever it is.

271
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
I think I got that sense from you.

272
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,000
I'm just in an abundance of caution

273
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
because people are listening to us

274
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:37,000
and making sure that we drive that point home.

275
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:40,000
What is suggested in the question that we received?

276
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:42,000
we're not going to have that happen.

277
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:47,000
Patrol leaders, council or the boys may be real hard driving

278
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:51,000
gung-ho guys who are really doing it in a spirit of fun

279
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:57,000
and things. as long as it's not a tiresome routine

280
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:00,000
that they use to belittle or haze people,

281
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
I don't really have a problem with it.

282
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:05,000
but let's just be very, very careful

283
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
to differentiate between those numbers,

284
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:08,000
Absolutely.

285
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
There's a couple of hidden questions here.

286
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
The first one is: you know,

287
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:15,000
we talked about the advisability of the committee

288
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:18,000
trying to install a system of discipline

289
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
or a demerit system or something like that

290
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:23,000
and we agreed that that was not a good idea.

291
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:27,000
but is it a good idea to have such a demerit system

292
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:30,000
or a system of punishments or disciplines?

293
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:33,000
A demerit system doesn't have a place in scouting.

294
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
Scouting is moving forward,

295
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
Demerits are moving backwards.

296
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:40,000
If you think about it for a little while,

297
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:44,000
it's just not compatible with the overall way,

298
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:47,000
In particular some of the quotes you've had

299
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:50,000
in the last couple of things from Bade and Powell,

300
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:55,000
the last couple of posts that you've done about advancement.

301
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
scouting is moving forward.

302
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
Scouts go, they advance.

303
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
Scouts do not retreat.

304
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
They don't demerit,

305
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:04,000
They merit,

306
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:09,000
And that has to permeate everything you do.

307
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:12,000
If you start something like this,

308
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:18,000
you will end up in a mire, in a pit of mess.

309
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:22,000
There may be one or two places in the world

310
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:25,000
for that kind of thing, in certain places

311
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:31,000
where you're working with very serious life and death situations,

312
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:36,000
But in the case of education, moving ahead, advancing,

313
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
that's why advancement is called advancement.

314
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,000
I don't think it has any place.

315
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
That's my opinion.

316
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:46,000
It's calling somebody down in a very negative way too

317
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:50,000
and calling attention to them in a negative way,

318
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
and you really don't ever want to do that

319
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
and you always want to be very positive.

320
00:18:54,000 --> 00:19:00,000
So you want the positive reinforcement versus the negative. also,

321
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
Walter, you got anything on that

322
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:03,000
Demerits? No,

323
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:05,000
We're talking about progress

324
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:08,000
Other than it being amenical to the principles

325
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:11,000
that we want to be exercising and scouting.

326
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:15,000
the other thing is, if you create a rule book like that

327
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:18,000
now that makes you a policeman

328
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
where you're going to have to be,

329
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
you're going to spend an inordinate amount of time

330
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
and your youth leaders are going to spend

331
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:27,000
an inordinate amount of time policing a bunch of rules,

332
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:32,000
when the scout law and the scout oath seem to be very adequate

333
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
to that task.

334
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
That would be what I would put into it.

335
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:39,000
The second hidden question here is:

336
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:42,000
they're having a meeting and the scouts are doing this.

337
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
so what's going on at the meeting?

338
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:48,000
that is less interesting than playing on my phone.

339
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:51,000
Well, and you got two issues there.

340
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:55,000
You got the meeting may not be theirs,

341
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:57,000
the regular stuff we talk about,

342
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:00,000
and then you may have those one or two guys

343
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:04,000
for whatever reason, no matter what your meeting is.

344
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:08,000
If Harrison Ford showed up

345
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:09,000
and the circus put on a show,

346
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:11,000
they'd be looking at their phones.

347
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
There's going to be other indications

348
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:15,000
that they're disconnected.

349
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:17,000
It's not going to just be their phone,

350
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:19,000
it's going to be behavior:

351
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:21,000
not showing up for camp out,

352
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:25,000
it's not bringing. something else is going on

353
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:28,000
and eventually that's

354
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
along with any other kind of behavior.

355
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
that's when you have to.

356
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
they're going to probably have to sit down

357
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:35,000
with a scout master and say:

358
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:36,000
what's going on here?

359
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
We need to make a deal.

360
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:42,000
Are you here because you shouldn't be,

361
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
but your dad brings you?

362
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
Do I need to sit down with you and your dad?

363
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
Do we all need to chat?

364
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
And it won't be just the phone.

365
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
it'll be four things.

366
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,000
I've had scouts that have already checked out

367
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
their parents suit them up, bring them down

368
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
every Tuesday at seven o'clock.

369
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
and he checked out three months ago

370
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:03,000
and I know it and he knows it

371
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:07,000
Well. thankfully, ours has been very,

372
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:09,000
very short lived.

373
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:12,000
They're talking about some game

374
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:16,000
or some viral YouTube video they saw.

375
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
They haven't checked out.

376
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
it's that little.

377
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
they're 15 minutes up.

378
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:25,000
now they moved on and a little reminder

379
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:29,000
and they focus back for a little while longer.

380
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:33,000
Thankfully, I haven't had that problem yet.

381
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,000
If I get to the end of a meeting

382
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:38,000
and I'm listening to the Patrol Leaders Council

383
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
and I'm hearing several complaints

384
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
about people being distracted

385
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,000
or not paying attention,

386
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:46,000
then my question to them is always:

387
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:49,000
well, what were you doing? that was not that interesting.

388
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:51,000
Why weren't these guys engaged?

389
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:53,000
Sometimes, that's the question to ask.

390
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:55,000
They need to make a little bit of an adjustment

391
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:57,000
in their presentation.

392
00:21:57,000 --> 00:21:59,000
How are you going to fix it next week,

393
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:01,000
Right?

394
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:03,000
That's the basic thing.

395
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:05,000
Scouting is awesome.

396
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:07,000
Make it awesome.

397
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:09,000
How do you make it awesome?

398
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,000
I do it with a podcast.

399
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:13,000
Sorry,

400
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:16,000
I have not seen Gangum style from you.

401
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
Yes, they do.

402
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:21,000
I'm not really ready to see Gangum style from you.

403
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
If I had a clue what you were talking about,

404
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,000
Right?

405
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:27,000
I don't have any idea either.

406
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:29,000
Yeah,

407
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:33,000
Google Gangum style and you'll find out.

408
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:35,000
and you won't, or not.

409
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:37,000
you can do either one

410
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:39,000
Gangum.

411
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:41,000
Put a link to this in your podcast.

412
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:44,000
Gangum style? I don't think so.

413
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,000
Let me offer you this

414
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:49,000
and see. I want to know if you agree with me.

415
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:52,000
I don't think it is our responsibility

416
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
to punish scouts.

417
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,000
I think that's why they have parents.

418
00:22:56,000 --> 00:23:00,000
Parents can do a much better job of it than we can.

419
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:03,000
If I have a behavior problem,

420
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:06,000
I'm going to make parents aware of it

421
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:10,000
and say that we don't discipline and punish,

422
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
but you certainly may take that option

423
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:16,000
if you feel it's merited in this instance.

424
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,000
We went backpacking a couple of weeks ago

425
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,000
and I woke up Sunday morning

426
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:25,000
to hear my troop quartermaster pretty loudly.

427
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:28,000
I heard the word matches

428
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
and I heard the word flicking

429
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:34,000
and I heard the word tent and burned.

430
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:39,000
My quartermaster was a very unhappy fellow

431
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:45,000
and it was a little while before they were presented to me,

432
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:49,000
but these guys were flicking matches in the tent

433
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:51,000
and they managed to burn it-

434
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:54,000
Not terribly, but they managed to burn it-

435
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:57,000
and of course that was a pretty serious problem.

436
00:23:57,000 --> 00:24:00,000
I put it in the hands of their fathers.

437
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:04,000
when they came to pick them up from the trip.

438
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
I explained what happened

439
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:09,000
and I explained that we don't punish

440
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:11,000
and we don't discipline.

441
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:13,000
but they may take the opportunity

442
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:15,000
to see if they can figure out what happened

443
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:17,000
and they can figure out

444
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:20,000
how we wouldn't have to deal with it in the future.

445
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:23,000
I felt pretty good about handling it that way

446
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:27,000
and I didn't feel as though there was any pressure

447
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:31,000
on the troop to have to be the arbiters

448
00:24:31,000 --> 00:24:34,000
of some kind of punishment or something like that.

449
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:36,000
Well, did the behavior stop

450
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:38,000
when the quartermaster went ballistic on it?

451
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:41,000
Yes, well, I think the behavior had already stopped.

452
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:44,000
I think it was discovered,

453
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:46,000
So you didn't have to get in between

454
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:50,000
a dangerous situation and the issue.

455
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:54,000
Some kind of little piece of brain

456
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:57,000
finally engaged and a clue bird landed

457
00:24:57,000 --> 00:25:00,000
and they suddenly realized

458
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:03,000
what kind of condition they were in,

459
00:25:03,000 --> 00:25:05,000
and after you talked to their dads

460
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:08,000
then they were starting to get a clue

461
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:10,000
In that particular case.

462
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:13,000
that's exactly perfectly handled

463
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:17,000
and probably turned out in the long run

464
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:20,000
an excellent lesson all around

465
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:24,000
for the quartermaster and for the two scouts,

466
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:27,000
and maybe even the dads,

467
00:25:27,000 --> 00:25:30,000
Because they're going to own that tent with a hole in it,

468
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:32,000
with a burn spot in it, for a long time

469
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:34,000
and every time they look at it.

470
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:36,000
they'll remember what they did

471
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:38,000
that they shouldn't have been doing.

472
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:41,000
But yeah, I agree with what you've been saying,

473
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:44,000
that the troop really should not

474
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:47,000
get into the punishment business at all.

475
00:25:47,000 --> 00:25:49,000
Very proud of my quartermaster:

476
00:25:49,000 --> 00:25:52,000
He managed to express his displeasure

477
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:54,000
without losing his temper

478
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:57,000
and he did it in an unmistakable fashion.

479
00:25:57,000 --> 00:25:59,000
I have to say.

480
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:01,000
I was a quartermaster as a scout

481
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:04,000
and I remember opening up the closet

482
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:06,000
and pulling out some Dutch ovens

483
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:08,000
and finding out that there were

484
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:11,000
some Dutch ovens with ecosystems in them

485
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:17,000
Which I hit with a hose

486
00:26:17,000 --> 00:26:19,000
from a considerable distance.

487
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:22,000
I don't know what I said at the time,

488
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:25,000
but it wasn't worth many people around

489
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:30,000
Having the ability to dress him down

490
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:32,000
like you say he was doing

491
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:34,000
and not really losing his temper,

492
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:39,000
and that's a skill that will carry him far in life.

493
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:42,000
when he's able to tell people stuff like that-

494
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:44,000
and they'll thank him for doing it

495
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:49,000
Because he does it in such a good way,

496
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:51,000
They understand where he's coming from,

497
00:26:51,000 --> 00:26:55,000
but he doesn't belittle them.

498
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:58,000
I think he used the word stupid several times.

499
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:03,000
Well, if you leave shrimp in a Dutch oven

500
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:07,000
and in the closet in Louisiana,

501
00:27:07,000 --> 00:27:11,000
I mean stupid could apply.

502
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:19,000
Listen, we would certainly like to hear from you

503
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:21,000
and we would like to get you to come

504
00:27:21,000 --> 00:27:25,000
and join us on the next scout master panel discussion.

505
00:27:25,000 --> 00:27:27,000
We get together once a month

506
00:27:27,000 --> 00:27:29,000
and we can have you phone in when we record,

507
00:27:29,000 --> 00:27:31,000
and you're going to find out how to do that

508
00:27:31,000 --> 00:27:33,000
towards the end of the podcast here,

509
00:27:33,000 --> 00:27:35,000
But let me, before we go,

510
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:38,000
thank Larry for joining us this evening.

511
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:39,000
Thank you very much, Larry.

512
00:27:39,000 --> 00:27:40,000
